{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015GmQMpT4U55WTWgqa1NuZa","type":"tool_result","content":"744\t<!-- FIGURE: ch04-zenith-declaration-not-order -->\n745\t```\n746\t                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐\n747\t                    │    THE FILED DECLARATION    │\n748\t                    │    (what should be true)    │\n749\t                    └──────────────┬──────────────┘\n750\t                                   │\n751\t          ┌────────────┬───────────┼───────────┬────────────┐\n752\t          │            │           │           │            │\n753\t      consulted    consulted   consulted   consulted    consulted\n754\t       at 09:00     at 09:04    at 09:04    at 11:20     at 03:17\n755\t          │            │           │           │            │\n756\t          ▼            ▼           ▼           ▼            ▼\n757\t     ( a scheduler )( a controller )( a kubelet )( a controller )( a controller )\n758\t\n759\t     No hand receives an instruction.\n760\t     Every hand reads the record, observes the world, and closes the gap.\n761\t```\n762\t\n763\t*Figure: the record outranks the moment it was written. Nothing in this figure is a command being passed down a chain; every arrow is a reading.*\n764\t\n765\t### ☀️ Zenith\n766\t\n767\t> ☀️ **Zenith:** The loop Chapter 3 showed you as an architecture diagram now has an input you know how to write. That was the promise at the end of Chapter 3 *[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §6 — the control loop, and the field it compares against]*, and this is the shape of it: **you do not participate in the loop. You supply its reference.**\n768\t>\n769\t> The system reads your declaration and observes the world. Wherever they disagree, it acts. It does this at 09:00 and at 03:17 and on the fourteen-thousandth iteration, with exactly the same logic, without you present, without needing to know what you did last time, without a workflow to resume. Your record is not a message that was delivered. It is the standing answer to a question the cluster asks itself continuously.\n770\t\n771\tName what that architecture buys you, because a distinction without a payoff is just trivia.\n772\t\n773\tA system that takes descriptions rather than commands can accept the same description twice with no harm done: the second submission describes the same world as the first, so there is nothing to correct. It can be told what should be true by someone who does not know what is currently true, because working out the difference is the system's job, not the author's. And it can keep that description in a file, which means the description outlives the terminal session that produced it, the person who typed it, and the incident that prompted it. Chapter 3 put it in the documentation's words and it lands harder now: *it shouldn't matter how you get from A to C* [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23].\n774\t\n775\tThose three properties compose into something, and this book will spend a later chapter on it. Not yet.\n776\t\n777\t### The honest correction\n778\t\n779\tOne more time, precisely, because §1 promised it and because slogans deserve auditing.\n780\t\n781\tKubernetes takes imperative instruction all day. `kubectl delete` deletes. `kubectl exec` runs a command inside a container. `kubectl port-forward` forwards a port. None of those has a declarative reading; they are pure imperatives and they are entirely normal to use [source: k8s-docs-kubectl-overview-2026-08-23].\n782\t\n783\tThe accurate claim is narrower than the chapter subtitle, and better:\n784\t\n785\t**The objects are declarations, and the imperative commands work by changing declarations.** `kubectl scale` updates the size of a workload [source: k8s-docs-kubectl-overview-2026-08-23], which is to say it edits a number in a `spec`, and then something else notices. The imperative command did not scale anything. It amended a record, and the loop did the rest.\n786\t\n787\tThat is the same discipline Chapter 3 applied to \"nobody is in charge.\" Both statements are true in the way that matters and false if you press them literally, and knowing which is which is the difference between understanding a system and reciting a slogan about it.\n788\t\n789\t---\n790\t\n791\t## Exam Alert! 🚨\n792\t\n793\t**High-Priority Topics**\n794\t\n795\t1. **The four required manifest fields:** `apiVersion`, `kind`, `metadata`, `spec`. By name, and what each supplies. The most mechanically checkable fact in this competency.\n796\t2. **`spec` versus `status`:** which you set, which the system supplies and updates.\n797\t3. **Namespaced versus cluster-scoped**, with Nodes, PersistentVolumes, and StorageClasses as the named cluster-scoped examples, and namespace objects themselves as the one candidates miss.\n798\t4. **The four initial namespaces** and what each is for. `kube-node-lease` is the forgotten one, and its purpose (Lease objects for node heartbeats) is the detail that gets asked.\n799\t5. **Labels versus annotations:** identifying and selectable, versus non-identifying and not.\n800\t6. **ConfigMap versus Secret:** a difference of intent and handling, not of encryption.\n801\t\n802\t**Common Traps**\n803\t\n804\t| Trap | Correct understanding |\n805\t|---|---|\n806\t| Using a namespace to separate two versions of the same software | Use labels within one namespace. Namespaces scope names; labels partition sets [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23] |\n807\t| \"Everything lives in a namespace\" | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespaces themselves do not [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23] |\n808\t| Confusing labels with annotations | Labels identify and can be selected on; annotations record and cannot [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23] |\n809\t| \"ConfigMaps hold config; Secrets hold *secure* config\" | Secrets are stored unencrypted by default [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23]. The difference is treatment, not cryptography |\n810\t| Assuming a ConfigMap change reaches a running container | Only via the API-reading path. The other three are applied by the kubelet at container launch [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n811\t| \"An immutable ConfigMap can be un-marked\" | It cannot be reverted. Delete and recreate [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n812\t| Forgetting the ConfigMap size ceiling | 1 MiB [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n813\t| Referencing a ConfigMap across namespaces | The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n814\t\n815\tFive of those eight live in §4, which is why that section carries the chapter's densest warning block and its highest attention cost. If you are budgeting revision time, §4 is where it goes.\n816\t\n817\t---\n818\t\n819\t## Practice Questions\n820\t\n821\tNineteen questions. Three test material from earlier chapters and are tagged as such. Four require two sections at once, because single-section recall is not what this exam is measuring.\n822\t\n823\t---\n824\t\n825\t**Q1.** **[retrieval: ch3]** You run `kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml`. Which component receives the request, and where does the resulting object live?\n826\t\n827\tA) The kubelet receives it and writes the object to disk on the node\n828\tB) The kube-scheduler receives it and holds it in its scheduling queue\n829\tC) The kube-apiserver receives it and etcd stores it\n830\tD) The kube-controller-manager receives it directly and stores it in memory\n831\t\n832\t**Q2.** **[retrieval: ch3]** You change one number in a manifest's `spec` and re-apply it. What happens to the control loop as a result?\n833\t\n834\tA) The loop is restarted with the new value as its starting condition\n835\tB) Nothing until you also run a command telling the controller to reconcile\n836\tC) The controller now observes a difference between `spec` and `status` and acts to close it\n837\tD) The previous loop terminates and a new loop is created for the new desired state\n838\t\n839\t**Q3.** Which of the following is **not** one of the four fields you must set in a manifest?\n840\t\n841\tA) `apiVersion`\n842\tB) `kind`\n843\tC) `status`\n844\tD) `metadata`\n845\t\n846\t**Q4.** An object's `status` block shows values that do not match what you wrote in that object's `spec`. Who wrote the `status` block?\n847\t\n848\tA) You did, when you applied the manifest\n849\tB) The Kubernetes system and its components\n850\tC) `kubectl`, when it rendered the object for display\n851\tD) Nobody — `status` is computed on read and not stored\n852\t\n853\t**Q5.** Which statement about `apiVersion` is correct?\n854\t\n855\tA) It names the version of the Kubernetes cluster the object was created on\n856\tB) It names the version of the API being used to create this object, and it appears once per object\n857\tC) It names the version of the API being used, and applies to the whole file\n858\tD) It is optional for built-in resource types and required only for custom resources\n859\t\n860\t---\n861\t\n862\t**Q6.** Which of these is cluster-scoped?\n863\t\n864\tA) ConfigMap\n865\tB) ServiceAccount\n866\tC) PersistentVolume\n867\tD) Deployment\n868\t\n869\t**Q7.** Your team wants to run `v2.3` and `v2.4` of the same application side by side in one cluster. What does the Kubernetes documentation direct you to do?\n870\t\n871\tA) Create a namespace per version — this is the documented use of namespaces\n872\tB) Keep both in one namespace and distinguish them with labels\n873\tC) Create a namespace per version and nest both inside a parent namespace\n874\tD) Create one namespace per version, then use a ResourceQuota to link them\n875\t\n876\t**Q8.** What is the `kube-node-lease` namespace for?\n877\t\n878\tA) Holding leases that grant temporary API access to nodes\n879\tB) Holding Lease objects associated with each node, which the kubelet uses to send heartbeats\n880\tC) Holding the scheduling reservations that bind Pods to nodes\n881\tD) Holding objects created by the Kubernetes system\n882\t\n883\t**Q9.** Which statement about namespaces is correct?\n884\t\n885\tA) Namespaces can be nested up to three levels deep\n886\tB) A resource can belong to multiple namespaces if it is referenced from both\n887\tC) Namespaces cannot be nested, and each resource is in exactly one namespace\n888\tD) Namespaces cannot be nested, but cluster-scoped resources belong to all of them simultaneously\n889\t\n890\t---\n891\t\n892\t**Q10.** What does using a Secret instead of a ConfigMap give you?\n893\t\n894\tA) Encryption of the data at rest, enabled by default\n895\tB) A distinct object type, a distinct access-control surface, and a defined place to attach encryption at rest — but no encryption by default\n896\tC) Encryption in transit between the API server and the kubelet, which ConfigMaps do not get\n897\tD) Automatic redaction so that no principal with API access can read the contents\n898\t\n899\t**Q11.** An application reads a setting from an environment variable populated by a ConfigMap. An operator edits the ConfigMap. What does the running application see?\n900\t\n901\tA) The new value immediately\n902\tB) The new value after a short kubelet resync interval\n903\tC) The old value — the kubelet used the ConfigMap data when it launched the container\n904\tD) An error, because the environment variable is now inconsistent with its source\n905\t\n906\t**Q12.** **[retrieval: ch2]** Chapter 2 established that container images are immutable: changing one means building a new image and recreating the container. ConfigMaps gained an analogous property in v1.19. Which statement about it is correct?\n907\t\n908\tA) An immutable ConfigMap can be edited by an administrator but not by an application\n909\tB) Immutability can be toggled off if the ConfigMap has no Pods consuming it\n910\tC) Once marked immutable, the change cannot be reverted and the data cannot be mutated — you can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap\n911\tD) Immutability applies to the `data` field but the `binaryData` field remains editable\n912\t\n913\t**Q13.** You need to make a 4 MB reference data file available to a containerized application. What does the documentation direct you to do?\n914\t\n915\tA) Split it across four ConfigMaps and mount all four\n916\tB) Store it in a Secret, which has no size limit\n917\tC) Store it in a ConfigMap — the 1 MiB figure is a recommendation, not a limit\n918\tD) Use a volume, a separate database, or a file service — ConfigMap data cannot exceed 1 MiB\n919\t\n920\t**Q14.** A Pod in namespace `team-a` needs configuration that already exists in a ConfigMap named `settings` in namespace `team-b`. What is your option?\n921\t\n922\tA) Reference it as `team-b/settings` from the Pod's manifest\n923\tB) Reference it by its fully qualified name, `settings.team-b.svc.cluster.local`\n924\tC) Create a ConfigMap named `settings` in `team-a` — the Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace\n925\tD) Grant the Pod's ServiceAccount read access to `team-b` and reference it normally\n926\t\n927\t---\n928\t\n929\t**Q15.** Which of these is a valid **set-based** selector?\n930\t\n931\tA) `environment != production`\n932\tB) `environment in (production, qa)`\n933\tC) `environment == production`\n934\tD) `environment = production`\n935\t\n936\t**Q16.** Given `matchLabels: {app: web}`, which `matchExpressions` entry is exactly equivalent?\n937\t\n938\tA) `{key: app, operator: Equals, values: [web]}`\n939\tB) `{key: app, operator: In, values: [web]}`\n940\tC) `{key: app, operator: Exists, values: [web]}`\n941\tD) `{key: app, operator: In, values: []}`\n942\t\n943\t**Q17.** A controller is written to manage every object carrying a particular attribute. The attribute has been recorded as an annotation. What happens?\n944\t\n945\tA) The controller finds them, but more slowly than if a label had been used\n946\tB) The controller cannot select them — selectors operate over labels, and annotations are non-identifying by definition\n947\tC) The controller finds them only if the annotation key uses the `kubernetes.io/` prefix\n948\tD) The controller finds them only within its own namespace\n949\t\n950\t**Q18.** A selector for `tier=frontend` returns three objects. You know that four objects in the cluster carry that exact label. What is the most likely explanation?\n951\t\n952\tA) One object's label was applied after creation, and post-creation labels are not indexed\n953\tB) The fourth object is in a different namespace than the one the query was scoped to\n954\tC) The fourth object also carries an annotation, which suppresses label matching\n955\tD) Equality-based selectors return a maximum of three results without pagination\n956\t\n957\t**Q19.** You are looking at a workload manifest that contains both a `spec.replicas` field and a `spec.selector.matchLabels` block. Which field determines *which* objects the workload manages, and which determines *how many* it maintains?\n958\t\n959\tA) `replicas` determines which; `matchLabels` determines how many\n960\tB) `matchLabels` determines which; `replicas` determines how many\n961\tC) `matchLabels` determines both — `replicas` is reported in `status` only\n962\tD) Neither — both are set by the system and reported in `status`\n963\t\n964\t---\n965\t\n966\t### Answers and Explanations\n967\t\n968\t**Q1. Answer: C.** Every read and write of cluster state goes through the kube-apiserver, and etcd is the consistent, highly-available key-value store used as Kubernetes' backing store for all cluster data [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23].\n969\t- **A** confuses the kubelet's role: it runs containers on a node from PodSpecs provided to it, and it is not a store of record.\n970\t- **B** picks a real control-plane component with the wrong job; the scheduler watches for Pods with no assigned node and selects nodes for them [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]. It receives nothing from `kubectl`.\n971\t- **D** describes the shape of the misconception that controllers are messaged directly. They are not; they watch the API server.\n972\t\n973\t**Q2. Answer: C.** A controller compares desired state against current state and acts on the difference [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23]; the difference is what changed, so the change is what it responds to.\n974\t- **A** and **D** both imagine the loop as something with a lifecycle tied to your action. It has neither a start you trigger nor an end. It is described as a non-terminating loop that regulates the state of a system [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23].\n975\t- **B** is the imperative instinct at its purest, and it is the single most useful wrong answer in this chapter. There is no such command, and needing one would defeat the entire architecture. Nobody tells the controller. The controller is already watching.\n976\t\n977\t**Q3. Answer: C.** The four fields to set are `apiVersion`, `kind`, `metadata`, and `spec` [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23]. `status` is supplied and updated by the Kubernetes system and its components; writing it accomplishes nothing.\n978\t\n979\t**Q4. Answer: B.** `status` describes the current state of the object, supplied and updated by the Kubernetes system and its components [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\n980\t- **A** inverts the authorship boundary.\n981\t- **C** is wrong: `status` is part of the stored object, not a display-time computation by the client.\n982\t- **D** is wrong for the same reason. It is a real, persisted field on the object.\n983\t\n984\t**Q5. Answer: B.** `apiVersion` is which version of the Kubernetes API you're using to create this object [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23], and it is one of the fields set per object.\n985\t- **A** conflates the API version of a resource type with the version of the cluster software. Those move independently; a resource's API version can remain stable across many cluster releases.\n986\t- **C** is the file-level assumption, and it fails because a single file can hold several objects of different kinds, each versioned independently.\n987\t- **D** is wrong: the four required fields are required for every object, built-in or custom.\n988\t\n989\t**Q6. Answer: C.** Low-level resources such as nodes and persistent volumes are not in any namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n990\t- **A** and **D** are among the documentation's own examples of namespaced objects.\n991\t- **B** is namespaced too, and pointedly so. ServiceAccounts are bound to a namespace, and every namespace gets a `default` one on creation [source: k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23].\n992\t\n993\t**Q7. Answer: B.** It is not necessary to use multiple namespaces to separate slightly different resources, such as different versions of the same software; use labels to distinguish resources within the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n994\t- **A** is the intuitive habit the documentation specifically corrects.\n995\t- **C** additionally fails on nesting: namespaces cannot be nested inside one another [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n996\t- **D** misuses ResourceQuota, which divides cluster resources between namespaces rather than linking them.\n997\t\n998\t**Q8. Answer: B.** `kube-node-lease` holds Lease objects associated with each node; node leases allow the kubelet to send heartbeats so that the control plane can detect node failure [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n999\t- **A** invents an authorization mechanism; leases here are liveness signals, not credentials.\n1000\t- **C** describes binding, which is the scheduler notifying the API server of its placement decision, a different mechanism entirely.\n1001\t- **D** is the definition of `kube-system`, and picking it means the four initial namespaces have blurred together. They are worth separating.\n1002\t\n1003\t**Q9. Answer: C.** Namespaces cannot be nested inside one another, and each Kubernetes resource can only be in one namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n1004\t- **A** and **B** contradict those two rules directly.\n1005\t- **D** is the subtle one and it is still wrong: cluster-scoped resources do not belong to every namespace. They belong to none. That distinction matters enormously for permissions, because \"in all namespaces\" and \"in no namespace\" imply very different things about who can be granted access *[cross-bearing: see Ch 12 §3]*.\n1006\t\n1007\t**Q10. Answer: B.** Kubernetes Secrets are, by default, stored unencrypted in the API server's underlying data store; anyone with API access can retrieve or modify one, as can anyone with etcd access or the ability to create a Pod in the namespace [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23]. What the object type gives you is a place to apply the four recommended protections, not the protections themselves.\n1008\t- **A** is the misconception this chapter exists to correct.\n1009\t- **C** is wrong on the premise: API traffic is TLS-protected as a matter of cluster configuration [source: k8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23], and that protection is not specific to Secrets.\n1010\t- **D** contradicts the documented behavior outright.\n1011\t\n1012\t**Q11. Answer: C.** For the environment-variable path, as for command-and-args and read-only volume file paths, the kubelet uses the data from the ConfigMap when it launches the containers for a Pod [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Only the API-reading path subscribes to updates.\n1013\t- **A** and **B** both assume a propagation mechanism that does not exist for this path.\n1014\t- **D** imagines a consistency check the platform does not perform; the container simply holds the value it was launched with.\n1015\t\n1016\t**Q12. Answer: C.** Once a ConfigMap is marked as immutable, it is not possible to revert this change nor to mutate the contents of the `data` or `binaryData` fields; you can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Note the parallel to Chapter 2's image rule: the response to \"this needs to change\" is *make a new one*, not *edit this one* [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n1017\t- **A** invents a privilege distinction. Immutability is a property of the object, not of the caller.\n1018\t- **B** is the most tempting wrong answer because it sounds like a reasonable safety valve. There is no such escape hatch.\n1019\t- **D** is wrong: both fields are covered.\n1020\t\n1021\t**Q13. Answer: D.** The data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed 1 MiB; for larger settings, consider mounting a volume or using a separate database or file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n1022\t- **A** is a workaround for a limit that exists precisely because ConfigMaps are not designed to hold large chunks of data; it fights the design rather than following the guidance.\n1023\t- **B** is wrong on its premise: a Secret is described as holding *a small amount* of sensitive data [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23], and is not the escape hatch for size.\n1024\t- **C** misreads the limit as advisory.\n1025\t\n1026\t**Q14. Answer: C.** The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. The configuration has to exist in `team-a`.\n1027\t- **A** invents a cross-namespace reference syntax that the field does not accept.\n1028\t- **B** borrows the Service DNS form, which addresses Services over the network [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. A ConfigMap is not reached over the network by a Pod's config machinery, and these are two mechanisms that look similar and are not.\n1029\t- **D** is the closest to plausible and still wrong: permission to read an object is not the same as the ability to reference it as a config source. Access control governs *who may*, not *what the field accepts*.\n1030\t\n1031\t**Q15. Answer: B.** Set-based selectors use `in`, `notin`, and `exists` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n1032\t- **A**, **C**, and **D** are all equality-based: `=`, `==`, and `!=` are the equality-based operators. `!=` in particular reads like a set operation and is not one.\n1033\t\n1034\t**Q16. Answer: B.** `matchLabels` is a map of `{key, value}` pairs equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n1035\t- **A** uses an operator that does not exist in this vocabulary; the operators are `In`, `NotIn`, `Exists`, and `DoesNotExist`.\n1036\t- **C** is a real operator with a different meaning: `Exists` tests for the key regardless of value, so it would also match `app: api`.\n1037\t- **D** is `In` with an empty value list, which matches nothing.\n1038\t\n1039\t**Q17. Answer: B.** Non-identifying information should be recorded using annotations [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]; selection is a label operation, and the label selector is the core grouping primitive [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. The fix is not to work around the selector. It is to record the attribute as a label, because wanting to select on it is what makes it identifying.\n1040\t- **A** implies a performance difference where there is a capability difference.\n1041\t- **C** inverts the prefix rule: `kubernetes.io/` and `k8s.io/` are reserved for core components, not required of user keys.\n1042\t- **D** describes namespace scoping, which is real but is not what stops the controller here.\n1043\t\n1044\t**Q18. Answer: B.** Selection operates within the namespace scope of the query; a namespaced query does not cross the boundary [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n1045\t- **A** contradicts the documented behavior: labels can be attached at creation time and subsequently added and modified at any time, with no difference in how they select [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n1046\t- **C** invents an interaction between annotations and labels. They are independent maps.\n1047\t- **D** invents a result limit.\n1048\t\n1049\t**Q19. Answer: B.** `matchLabels` is a selector, and the label selector is the core grouping primitive; it answers *which objects* [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. `replicas` sits in `spec` and states desired state: how many should exist, which is exactly the documentation's own worked example of a Deployment spec specifying three replicas [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\n1050\t- **A** inverts the two.\n1051\t- **C** and **D** both misplace authorship: `replicas` is something you set in `spec`, not something reported to you in `status`. `status` reports how many *currently* exist, which is the other half of the pair and the thing the controller compares against.\n1052\t\n1053\t---\n1054\t\n1055\t## Chapter Summary\n1056\t\n1057\t| Concept | Remember This |\n1058\t|---|---|\n1059\t| Kubernetes object | A persistent entity, a \"record of intent.\" Create it and the system constantly works to ensure it exists [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23] |\n1060\t| The four fields | `apiVersion`, `kind`, `metadata`, `spec`. Every object. Every time. Which API, which kind, which one, what it should look like |\n1061\t| `spec` | What you want. You write it |\n1062\t| `status` | What is. The system writes it. Every controller exists to close the distance between the two |\n1063\t| Manifest | The file you write the object in. YAML by convention. Submit with `kubectl apply -f` |\n1064\t| Namespace | A scope for names. Unique within, not across. Cannot be nested. One namespace per resource |\n1065\t| Initial namespaces | `default` (start without creating one), `kube-system` (system objects), `kube-public` (readable by all, by convention), `kube-node-lease` (node heartbeat Leases) |\n1066\t| Cluster-scoped | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespace objects themselves. `kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false` settles it |\n1067\t| ConfigMap | Non-confidential key-value data, ≤1 MiB, same namespace as the Pod. Four consumption paths; only the API-reading one sees updates. Immutability is irreversible |\n1068\t| Secret | Same shape, different intent and treatment. Stored unencrypted by default. Readable by anyone with API access, etcd access, or Pod-creation rights in the namespace |\n1069\t| `dockerconfigjson` | The Secret type behind `imagePullSecrets`, a serialized `~/.docker/config.json` |\n1070\t| Label | Key/value identifying attribute. Selectable. Means nothing to the core system, which is why it works everywhere |\n1071\t| Label selector | **The core grouping primitive in Kubernetes.** Equality-based (`=`, `==`, `!=`) and set-based (`in`, `notin`, `exists`), ANDed with commas |\n1072\t| `matchLabels` | Exactly equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` |\n1073\t| Annotation | Non-identifying metadata. Recordable, not selectable. If you might search on it, it's a label |\n1074\t\n1075\t---\n1076\t\n1077\t## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n1078\t\n1079\t**Voyage Progress:** 🗺️ → 🌊 → 🌅 — Chapter 4 of 15 complete. You have crossed from *watching the system* to *writing to it*, which is the largest single step in this book.\n1080\t\n1081\tYou can now read a manifest you have never seen. You know which of its fields you author and which one is a report. You know where its name has to be unique and which resources have no name-scope at all. You know how to describe a set of objects rather than list one. And you know the exact thing a Secret does and does not do, which is a piece of knowledge a surprising number of working practitioners have wrong.\n1082\t\n1083\t---\n1084\t\n1085\t## The Voyage Ahead\n1086\t\n1087\tYou have written declarations for five kinds of thing. You have not yet written one for the thing that actually runs.\n1088\t\n1089\tChapter 5 is about the Pod: the object that represents a set of one or more running containers on your cluster, and the unit that everything else in Kubernetes ultimately arranges [source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23]. Everything you learned here applies to it unchanged: four fields, a `spec` you write, a `status` you read. But a Pod's `status` has something the objects in this chapter did not: a **phase**, a small vocabulary of words describing where in its short life the Pod currently is. And that vocabulary is the first thing you look at when something is wrong.\n1090\t\n1091\tThere is a second thing to watch for. A Pod is described in the documentation as *relatively ephemeral rather than durable*, and it is never rescheduled to a different node. If the node it is on dies, it is not moved; it is replaced by a new, near-identical Pod with a different UID [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. Hold that against what you learned in §1. If the thing that runs your application is designed to be replaced rather than repaired, then a declaration that names a specific Pod is a declaration about something disposable, and something else must be holding the intent that survives it.\n1092\t\n1093\tChapter 5 introduces the disposable thing. Chapter 6 introduces what holds the intent.\n1094\t\n1095\t> *\"You have stopped issuing orders. Everything after this is learning what reads them.\"*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f79c78d6-dfaa-4a26-88db-2b1ab3828c08","uuid":"5aa1a526-bf4f-4f6c-b2d9-19d8c1d1082c","timestamp":"2026-08-24T09:05:41.898Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-04\\draft-v1.md","content":"<!-- FIGURE: ch04-zenith-declaration-not-order -->\n```\n                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐\n                    │    THE FILED DECLARATION    │\n                    │    (what should be true)    │\n                    └──────────────┬──────────────┘\n                                   │\n          ┌────────────┬───────────┼───────────┬────────────┐\n          │            │           │           │            │\n      consulted    consulted   consulted   consulted    consulted\n       at 09:00     at 09:04    at 09:04    at 11:20     at 03:17\n          │            │           │           │            │\n          ▼            ▼           ▼           ▼            ▼\n     ( a scheduler )( a controller )( a kubelet )( a controller )( a controller )\n\n     No hand receives an instruction.\n     Every hand reads the record, observes the world, and closes the gap.\n```\n\n*Figure: the record outranks the moment it was written. Nothing in this figure is a command being passed down a chain; every arrow is a reading.*\n\n### ☀️ Zenith\n\n> ☀️ **Zenith:** The loop Chapter 3 showed you as an architecture diagram now has an input you know how to write. That was the promise at the end of Chapter 3 *[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §6 — the control loop, and the field it compares against]*, and this is the shape of it: **you do not participate in the loop. You supply its reference.**\n>\n> The system reads your declaration and observes the world. Wherever they disagree, it acts. It does this at 09:00 and at 03:17 and on the fourteen-thousandth iteration, with exactly the same logic, without you present, without needing to know what you did last time, without a workflow to resume. Your record is not a message that was delivered. It is the standing answer to a question the cluster asks itself continuously.\n\nName what that architecture buys you, because a distinction without a payoff is just trivia.\n\nA system that takes descriptions rather than commands can accept the same description twice with no harm done: the second submission describes the same world as the first, so there is nothing to correct. It can be told what should be true by someone who does not know what is currently true, because working out the difference is the system's job, not the author's. And it can keep that description in a file, which means the description outlives the terminal session that produced it, the person who typed it, and the incident that prompted it. Chapter 3 put it in the documentation's words and it lands harder now: *it shouldn't matter how you get from A to C* [source: k8s-docs-overview-2026-08-23].\n\nThose three properties compose into something, and this book will spend a later chapter on it. Not yet.\n\n### The honest correction\n\nOne more time, precisely, because §1 promised it and because slogans deserve auditing.\n\nKubernetes takes imperative instruction all day. `kubectl delete` deletes. `kubectl exec` runs a command inside a container. `kubectl port-forward` forwards a port. None of those has a declarative reading; they are pure imperatives and they are entirely normal to use [source: k8s-docs-kubectl-overview-2026-08-23].\n\nThe accurate claim is narrower than the chapter subtitle, and better:\n\n**The objects are declarations, and the imperative commands work by changing declarations.** `kubectl scale` updates the size of a workload [source: k8s-docs-kubectl-overview-2026-08-23], which is to say it edits a number in a `spec`, and then something else notices. The imperative command did not scale anything. It amended a record, and the loop did the rest.\n\nThat is the same discipline Chapter 3 applied to \"nobody is in charge.\" Both statements are true in the way that matters and false if you press them literally, and knowing which is which is the difference between understanding a system and reciting a slogan about it.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Alert! 🚨\n\n**High-Priority Topics**\n\n1. **The four required manifest fields:** `apiVersion`, `kind`, `metadata`, `spec`. By name, and what each supplies. The most mechanically checkable fact in this competency.\n2. **`spec` versus `status`:** which you set, which the system supplies and updates.\n3. **Namespaced versus cluster-scoped**, with Nodes, PersistentVolumes, and StorageClasses as the named cluster-scoped examples, and namespace objects themselves as the one candidates miss.\n4. **The four initial namespaces** and what each is for. `kube-node-lease` is the forgotten one, and its purpose (Lease objects for node heartbeats) is the detail that gets asked.\n5. **Labels versus annotations:** identifying and selectable, versus non-identifying and not.\n6. **ConfigMap versus Secret:** a difference of intent and handling, not of encryption.\n\n**Common Traps**\n\n| Trap | Correct understanding |\n|---|---|\n| Using a namespace to separate two versions of the same software | Use labels within one namespace. Namespaces scope names; labels partition sets [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23] |\n| \"Everything lives in a namespace\" | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespaces themselves do not [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23] |\n| Confusing labels with annotations | Labels identify and can be selected on; annotations record and cannot [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23] |\n| \"ConfigMaps hold config; Secrets hold *secure* config\" | Secrets are stored unencrypted by default [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23]. The difference is treatment, not cryptography |\n| Assuming a ConfigMap change reaches a running container | Only via the API-reading path. The other three are applied by the kubelet at container launch [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n| \"An immutable ConfigMap can be un-marked\" | It cannot be reverted. Delete and recreate [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n| Forgetting the ConfigMap size ceiling | 1 MiB [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n| Referencing a ConfigMap across namespaces | The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23] |\n\nFive of those eight live in §4, which is why that section carries the chapter's densest warning block and its highest attention cost. If you are budgeting revision time, §4 is where it goes.\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions\n\nNineteen questions. Three test material from earlier chapters and are tagged as such. Four require two sections at once, because single-section recall is not what this exam is measuring.\n\n---\n\n**Q1.** **[retrieval: ch3]** You run `kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml`. Which component receives the request, and where does the resulting object live?\n\nA) The kubelet receives it and writes the object to disk on the node\nB) The kube-scheduler receives it and holds it in its scheduling queue\nC) The kube-apiserver receives it and etcd stores it\nD) The kube-controller-manager receives it directly and stores it in memory\n\n**Q2.** **[retrieval: ch3]** You change one number in a manifest's `spec` and re-apply it. What happens to the control loop as a result?\n\nA) The loop is restarted with the new value as its starting condition\nB) Nothing until you also run a command telling the controller to reconcile\nC) The controller now observes a difference between `spec` and `status` and acts to close it\nD) The previous loop terminates and a new loop is created for the new desired state\n\n**Q3.** Which of the following is **not** one of the four fields you must set in a manifest?\n\nA) `apiVersion`\nB) `kind`\nC) `status`\nD) `metadata`\n\n**Q4.** An object's `status` block shows values that do not match what you wrote in that object's `spec`. Who wrote the `status` block?\n\nA) You did, when you applied the manifest\nB) The Kubernetes system and its components\nC) `kubectl`, when it rendered the object for display\nD) Nobody — `status` is computed on read and not stored\n\n**Q5.** Which statement about `apiVersion` is correct?\n\nA) It names the version of the Kubernetes cluster the object was created on\nB) It names the version of the API being used to create this object, and it appears once per object\nC) It names the version of the API being used, and applies to the whole file\nD) It is optional for built-in resource types and required only for custom resources\n\n---\n\n**Q6.** Which of these is cluster-scoped?\n\nA) ConfigMap\nB) ServiceAccount\nC) PersistentVolume\nD) Deployment\n\n**Q7.** Your team wants to run `v2.3` and `v2.4` of the same application side by side in one cluster. What does the Kubernetes documentation direct you to do?\n\nA) Create a namespace per version — this is the documented use of namespaces\nB) Keep both in one namespace and distinguish them with labels\nC) Create a namespace per version and nest both inside a parent namespace\nD) Create one namespace per version, then use a ResourceQuota to link them\n\n**Q8.** What is the `kube-node-lease` namespace for?\n\nA) Holding leases that grant temporary API access to nodes\nB) Holding Lease objects associated with each node, which the kubelet uses to send heartbeats\nC) Holding the scheduling reservations that bind Pods to nodes\nD) Holding objects created by the Kubernetes system\n\n**Q9.** Which statement about namespaces is correct?\n\nA) Namespaces can be nested up to three levels deep\nB) A resource can belong to multiple namespaces if it is referenced from both\nC) Namespaces cannot be nested, and each resource is in exactly one namespace\nD) Namespaces cannot be nested, but cluster-scoped resources belong to all of them simultaneously\n\n---\n\n**Q10.** What does using a Secret instead of a ConfigMap give you?\n\nA) Encryption of the data at rest, enabled by default\nB) A distinct object type, a distinct access-control surface, and a defined place to attach encryption at rest — but no encryption by default\nC) Encryption in transit between the API server and the kubelet, which ConfigMaps do not get\nD) Automatic redaction so that no principal with API access can read the contents\n\n**Q11.** An application reads a setting from an environment variable populated by a ConfigMap. An operator edits the ConfigMap. What does the running application see?\n\nA) The new value immediately\nB) The new value after a short kubelet resync interval\nC) The old value — the kubelet used the ConfigMap data when it launched the container\nD) An error, because the environment variable is now inconsistent with its source\n\n**Q12.** **[retrieval: ch2]** Chapter 2 established that container images are immutable: changing one means building a new image and recreating the container. ConfigMaps gained an analogous property in v1.19. Which statement about it is correct?\n\nA) An immutable ConfigMap can be edited by an administrator but not by an application\nB) Immutability can be toggled off if the ConfigMap has no Pods consuming it\nC) Once marked immutable, the change cannot be reverted and the data cannot be mutated — you can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap\nD) Immutability applies to the `data` field but the `binaryData` field remains editable\n\n**Q13.** You need to make a 4 MB reference data file available to a containerized application. What does the documentation direct you to do?\n\nA) Split it across four ConfigMaps and mount all four\nB) Store it in a Secret, which has no size limit\nC) Store it in a ConfigMap — the 1 MiB figure is a recommendation, not a limit\nD) Use a volume, a separate database, or a file service — ConfigMap data cannot exceed 1 MiB\n\n**Q14.** A Pod in namespace `team-a` needs configuration that already exists in a ConfigMap named `settings` in namespace `team-b`. What is your option?\n\nA) Reference it as `team-b/settings` from the Pod's manifest\nB) Reference it by its fully qualified name, `settings.team-b.svc.cluster.local`\nC) Create a ConfigMap named `settings` in `team-a` — the Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace\nD) Grant the Pod's ServiceAccount read access to `team-b` and reference it normally\n\n---\n\n**Q15.** Which of these is a valid **set-based** selector?\n\nA) `environment != production`\nB) `environment in (production, qa)`\nC) `environment == production`\nD) `environment = production`\n\n**Q16.** Given `matchLabels: {app: web}`, which `matchExpressions` entry is exactly equivalent?\n\nA) `{key: app, operator: Equals, values: [web]}`\nB) `{key: app, operator: In, values: [web]}`\nC) `{key: app, operator: Exists, values: [web]}`\nD) `{key: app, operator: In, values: []}`\n\n**Q17.** A controller is written to manage every object carrying a particular attribute. The attribute has been recorded as an annotation. What happens?\n\nA) The controller finds them, but more slowly than if a label had been used\nB) The controller cannot select them — selectors operate over labels, and annotations are non-identifying by definition\nC) The controller finds them only if the annotation key uses the `kubernetes.io/` prefix\nD) The controller finds them only within its own namespace\n\n**Q18.** A selector for `tier=frontend` returns three objects. You know that four objects in the cluster carry that exact label. What is the most likely explanation?\n\nA) One object's label was applied after creation, and post-creation labels are not indexed\nB) The fourth object is in a different namespace than the one the query was scoped to\nC) The fourth object also carries an annotation, which suppresses label matching\nD) Equality-based selectors return a maximum of three results without pagination\n\n**Q19.** You are looking at a workload manifest that contains both a `spec.replicas` field and a `spec.selector.matchLabels` block. Which field determines *which* objects the workload manages, and which determines *how many* it maintains?\n\nA) `replicas` determines which; `matchLabels` determines how many\nB) `matchLabels` determines which; `replicas` determines how many\nC) `matchLabels` determines both — `replicas` is reported in `status` only\nD) Neither — both are set by the system and reported in `status`\n\n---\n\n### Answers and Explanations\n\n**Q1. Answer: C.** Every read and write of cluster state goes through the kube-apiserver, and etcd is the consistent, highly-available key-value store used as Kubernetes' backing store for all cluster data [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23].\n- **A** confuses the kubelet's role: it runs containers on a node from PodSpecs provided to it, and it is not a store of record.\n- **B** picks a real control-plane component with the wrong job; the scheduler watches for Pods with no assigned node and selects nodes for them [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]. It receives nothing from `kubectl`.\n- **D** describes the shape of the misconception that controllers are messaged directly. They are not; they watch the API server.\n\n**Q2. Answer: C.** A controller compares desired state against current state and acts on the difference [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23]; the difference is what changed, so the change is what it responds to.\n- **A** and **D** both imagine the loop as something with a lifecycle tied to your action. It has neither a start you trigger nor an end. It is described as a non-terminating loop that regulates the state of a system [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23].\n- **B** is the imperative instinct at its purest, and it is the single most useful wrong answer in this chapter. There is no such command, and needing one would defeat the entire architecture. Nobody tells the controller. The controller is already watching.\n\n**Q3. Answer: C.** The four fields to set are `apiVersion`, `kind`, `metadata`, and `spec` [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23]. `status` is supplied and updated by the Kubernetes system and its components; writing it accomplishes nothing.\n\n**Q4. Answer: B.** `status` describes the current state of the object, supplied and updated by the Kubernetes system and its components [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\n- **A** inverts the authorship boundary.\n- **C** is wrong: `status` is part of the stored object, not a display-time computation by the client.\n- **D** is wrong for the same reason. It is a real, persisted field on the object.\n\n**Q5. Answer: B.** `apiVersion` is which version of the Kubernetes API you're using to create this object [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23], and it is one of the fields set per object.\n- **A** conflates the API version of a resource type with the version of the cluster software. Those move independently; a resource's API version can remain stable across many cluster releases.\n- **C** is the file-level assumption, and it fails because a single file can hold several objects of different kinds, each versioned independently.\n- **D** is wrong: the four required fields are required for every object, built-in or custom.\n\n**Q6. Answer: C.** Low-level resources such as nodes and persistent volumes are not in any namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** and **D** are among the documentation's own examples of namespaced objects.\n- **B** is namespaced too, and pointedly so. ServiceAccounts are bound to a namespace, and every namespace gets a `default` one on creation [source: k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23].\n\n**Q7. Answer: B.** It is not necessary to use multiple namespaces to separate slightly different resources, such as different versions of the same software; use labels to distinguish resources within the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** is the intuitive habit the documentation specifically corrects.\n- **C** additionally fails on nesting: namespaces cannot be nested inside one another [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **D** misuses ResourceQuota, which divides cluster resources between namespaces rather than linking them.\n\n**Q8. Answer: B.** `kube-node-lease` holds Lease objects associated with each node; node leases allow the kubelet to send heartbeats so that the control plane can detect node failure [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** invents an authorization mechanism; leases here are liveness signals, not credentials.\n- **C** describes binding, which is the scheduler notifying the API server of its placement decision, a different mechanism entirely.\n- **D** is the definition of `kube-system`, and picking it means the four initial namespaces have blurred together. They are worth separating.\n\n**Q9. Answer: C.** Namespaces cannot be nested inside one another, and each Kubernetes resource can only be in one namespace [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** and **B** contradict those two rules directly.\n- **D** is the subtle one and it is still wrong: cluster-scoped resources do not belong to every namespace. They belong to none. That distinction matters enormously for permissions, because \"in all namespaces\" and \"in no namespace\" imply very different things about who can be granted access *[cross-bearing: see Ch 12 §3]*.\n\n**Q10. Answer: B.** Kubernetes Secrets are, by default, stored unencrypted in the API server's underlying data store; anyone with API access can retrieve or modify one, as can anyone with etcd access or the ability to create a Pod in the namespace [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23]. What the object type gives you is a place to apply the four recommended protections, not the protections themselves.\n- **A** is the misconception this chapter exists to correct.\n- **C** is wrong on the premise: API traffic is TLS-protected as a matter of cluster configuration [source: k8s-docs-cloud-native-security-2026-08-23], and that protection is not specific to Secrets.\n- **D** contradicts the documented behavior outright.\n\n**Q11. Answer: C.** For the environment-variable path, as for command-and-args and read-only volume file paths, the kubelet uses the data from the ConfigMap when it launches the containers for a Pod [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Only the API-reading path subscribes to updates.\n- **A** and **B** both assume a propagation mechanism that does not exist for this path.\n- **D** imagines a consistency check the platform does not perform; the container simply holds the value it was launched with.\n\n**Q12. Answer: C.** Once a ConfigMap is marked as immutable, it is not possible to revert this change nor to mutate the contents of the `data` or `binaryData` fields; you can only delete and recreate the ConfigMap [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. Note the parallel to Chapter 2's image rule: the response to \"this needs to change\" is *make a new one*, not *edit this one* [source: k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23].\n- **A** invents a privilege distinction. Immutability is a property of the object, not of the caller.\n- **B** is the most tempting wrong answer because it sounds like a reasonable safety valve. There is no such escape hatch.\n- **D** is wrong: both fields are covered.\n\n**Q13. Answer: D.** The data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed 1 MiB; for larger settings, consider mounting a volume or using a separate database or file service [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23].\n- **A** is a workaround for a limit that exists precisely because ConfigMaps are not designed to hold large chunks of data; it fights the design rather than following the guidance.\n- **B** is wrong on its premise: a Secret is described as holding *a small amount* of sensitive data [source: k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23], and is not the escape hatch for size.\n- **C** misreads the limit as advisory.\n\n**Q14. Answer: C.** The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace [source: k8s-docs-configmap-2026-08-23]. The configuration has to exist in `team-a`.\n- **A** invents a cross-namespace reference syntax that the field does not accept.\n- **B** borrows the Service DNS form, which addresses Services over the network [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23]. A ConfigMap is not reached over the network by a Pod's config machinery, and these are two mechanisms that look similar and are not.\n- **D** is the closest to plausible and still wrong: permission to read an object is not the same as the ability to reference it as a config source. Access control governs *who may*, not *what the field accepts*.\n\n**Q15. Answer: B.** Set-based selectors use `in`, `notin`, and `exists` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n- **A**, **C**, and **D** are all equality-based: `=`, `==`, and `!=` are the equality-based operators. `!=` in particular reads like a set operation and is not one.\n\n**Q16. Answer: B.** `matchLabels` is a map of `{key, value}` pairs equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n- **A** uses an operator that does not exist in this vocabulary; the operators are `In`, `NotIn`, `Exists`, and `DoesNotExist`.\n- **C** is a real operator with a different meaning: `Exists` tests for the key regardless of value, so it would also match `app: api`.\n- **D** is `In` with an empty value list, which matches nothing.\n\n**Q17. Answer: B.** Non-identifying information should be recorded using annotations [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]; selection is a label operation, and the label selector is the core grouping primitive [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. The fix is not to work around the selector. It is to record the attribute as a label, because wanting to select on it is what makes it identifying.\n- **A** implies a performance difference where there is a capability difference.\n- **C** inverts the prefix rule: `kubernetes.io/` and `k8s.io/` are reserved for core components, not required of user keys.\n- **D** describes namespace scoping, which is real but is not what stops the controller here.\n\n**Q18. Answer: B.** Selection operates within the namespace scope of the query; a namespaced query does not cross the boundary [source: k8s-docs-namespaces-2026-08-23].\n- **A** contradicts the documented behavior: labels can be attached at creation time and subsequently added and modified at any time, with no difference in how they select [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23].\n- **C** invents an interaction between annotations and labels. They are independent maps.\n- **D** invents a result limit.\n\n**Q19. Answer: B.** `matchLabels` is a selector, and the label selector is the core grouping primitive; it answers *which objects* [source: k8s-docs-labels-selectors-2026-08-23]. `replicas` sits in `spec` and states desired state: how many should exist, which is exactly the documentation's own worked example of a Deployment spec specifying three replicas [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23].\n- **A** inverts the two.\n- **C** and **D** both misplace authorship: `replicas` is something you set in `spec`, not something reported to you in `status`. `status` reports how many *currently* exist, which is the other half of the pair and the thing the controller compares against.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter Summary\n\n| Concept | Remember This |\n|---|---|\n| Kubernetes object | A persistent entity, a \"record of intent.\" Create it and the system constantly works to ensure it exists [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23] |\n| The four fields | `apiVersion`, `kind`, `metadata`, `spec`. Every object. Every time. Which API, which kind, which one, what it should look like |\n| `spec` | What you want. You write it |\n| `status` | What is. The system writes it. Every controller exists to close the distance between the two |\n| Manifest | The file you write the object in. YAML by convention. Submit with `kubectl apply -f` |\n| Namespace | A scope for names. Unique within, not across. Cannot be nested. One namespace per resource |\n| Initial namespaces | `default` (start without creating one), `kube-system` (system objects), `kube-public` (readable by all, by convention), `kube-node-lease` (node heartbeat Leases) |\n| Cluster-scoped | Nodes, PersistentVolumes, StorageClasses, and namespace objects themselves. `kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false` settles it |\n| ConfigMap | Non-confidential key-value data, ≤1 MiB, same namespace as the Pod. Four consumption paths; only the API-reading one sees updates. Immutability is irreversible |\n| Secret | Same shape, different intent and treatment. Stored unencrypted by default. Readable by anyone with API access, etcd access, or Pod-creation rights in the namespace |\n| `dockerconfigjson` | The Secret type behind `imagePullSecrets`, a serialized `~/.docker/config.json` |\n| Label | Key/value identifying attribute. Selectable. Means nothing to the core system, which is why it works everywhere |\n| Label selector | **The core grouping primitive in Kubernetes.** Equality-based (`=`, `==`, `!=`) and set-based (`in`, `notin`, `exists`), ANDed with commas |\n| `matchLabels` | Exactly equivalent to `matchExpressions` with operator `In` |\n| Annotation | Non-identifying metadata. Recordable, not selectable. If you might search on it, it's a label |\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Safe Harbor\n\n**Voyage Progress:** 🗺️ → 🌊 → 🌅 — Chapter 4 of 15 complete. You have crossed from *watching the system* to *writing to it*, which is the largest single step in this book.\n\nYou can now read a manifest you have never seen. You know which of its fields you author and which one is a report. You know where its name has to be unique and which resources have no name-scope at all. You know how to describe a set of objects rather than list one. And you know the exact thing a Secret does and does not do, which is a piece of knowledge a surprising number of working practitioners have wrong.\n\n---\n\n## The Voyage Ahead\n\nYou have written declarations for five kinds of thing. You have not yet written one for the thing that actually runs.\n\nChapter 5 is about the Pod: the object that represents a set of one or more running containers on your cluster, and the unit that everything else in Kubernetes ultimately arranges [source: k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23]. Everything you learned here applies to it unchanged: four fields, a `spec` you write, a `status` you read. But a Pod's `status` has something the objects in this chapter did not: a **phase**, a small vocabulary of words describing where in its short life the Pod currently is. And that vocabulary is the first thing you look at when something is wrong.\n\nThere is a second thing to watch for. A Pod is described in the documentation as *relatively ephemeral rather than durable*, and it is never rescheduled to a different node. If the node it is on dies, it is not moved; it is replaced by a new, near-identical Pod with a different UID [source: k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23]. Hold that against what you learned in §1. If the thing that runs your application is designed to be replaced rather than repaired, then a declaration that names a specific Pod is a declaration about something disposable, and something else must be holding the intent that survives it.\n\nChapter 5 introduces the disposable thing. Chapter 6 introduces what holds the intent.\n\n> *\"You have stopped issuing orders. Everything after this is learning what reads them.\"*","numLines":352,"startLine":744,"totalLines":1095}}}
